p2u: 1
deck:
  title: How Music Affects Mood
  subtitle: Why a song can change how you feel in seconds
  theme: writebook
  meta:
    topic: Music and emotion
slides:
  - layout: title
    title: How Music Affects Mood
    subtitle: Why a song can change how you feel in seconds

  - layout: content
    title: Why Music Moves Us
    body: |
      Music is one of the few things that can change how you feel **before you
      even decide to feel it**. A few notes can lift you up or bring you down.

      - Your brain constantly predicts what comes next; music plays with that
      - When the prediction is *just* right, it rewards you with **dopamine**
      - That is why a chorus can give you goosebumps
    notes: |
      Dopamine is the same reward chemical involved in food and friendship.
      Music gives it to you for free.

  - layout: diagram
    title: From Sound to Feeling
    blocks:
      - kind: diagram
        lang: d2-sketch
        source: |
          direction: right
          sound: Sound
          brain: "Brain predicts"
          reward: "Dopamine"
          mood: Mood
          sound -> brain: hear it
          brain -> reward: "just right?"
          reward -> mood: "feel it"
    notes: The loop from hearing to feeling, simplified.

  - layout: content
    title: The Building Blocks
    body: |
      Every song is built from a handful of ingredients, and each one nudges
      your mood in its own direction.

      **Tempo** — the speed of the beat. Fast tempos tend to energise, slow
      tempos tend to calm or sadden. Around 120 BPM feels like a brisk walk;
      around 60 BPM feels like a resting heart.

      **Key and mode** — major keys usually sound bright and happy, minor keys
      usually sound wistful or serious. The same melody can flip meaning when
      you move it from major to minor.

      **Timbre** — the "colour" of the sound. A warm cello and a bright synth
      can play the same note and make you feel completely different things.
      Rough, distorted sounds read as intense; soft, breathy sounds read as
      intimate.

      **Dynamics** — how loud or quiet it gets, and how quickly it changes. A
      sudden swell feels like a rush; a slow fade feels like letting go.

      **Lyrics** — words give the feeling a story. A sad lyric over a happy
      tune can feel bittersweet, and a hopeful lyric over a minor chord can
      feel like courage.

      **Familiarity** — the more you know a song, the more it can move you.
      Memory and music are tightly linked, which is why a song from years ago
      can drop you straight back into a moment.

      None of these work alone. Mood is the sum of tempo, key, timbre,
      dynamics, lyrics and memory, all at once.
    notes: |
      This slide is intentionally long — it should scroll rather than be
      clipped or shrunk.

  - layout: two-column
    title: Tempo Changes Energy
    left: |
      **Faster (100–160 BPM)**

      - Workouts and dancing
      - Feels like momentum
      - Raises arousal and alertness
    right: |
      **Slower (50–80 BPM)**

      - Focus, sleep, reflection
      - Feels like calm or melancholy
      - Lowers heart rate and tension
    notes: Arousal here means alertness, not romance.

  - layout: compare
    title: Major vs Minor
    left: |
      **Major**

      - Bright, open, hopeful
      - Happy endings and celebrations
      - "Everything is fine"
    right: |
      **Minor**

      - Darker, inward, serious
      - Longing and reflection
      - "Something is unresolved"
    notes: Neither is better; they are two colours on the same palette.

  - layout: content
    title: Memory, Nostalgia and Comfort
    body: |
      Music is a time machine. Because it is stored with the memories around
      it, hearing a song can bring back a whole feeling, not just a sound.

      - Familiar songs feel safer and can lower stress
      - Shared music builds closeness — dancing and singing together syncs us
      - Nostalgia is bittersweet: warm and sad at the same time

  - layout: content
    title: A Practical Guide
    body: |
      You can use music on purpose, like a tool.

      - **To focus:** steady, low-lyric, mid-tempo music
      - **To lift your mood:** bright major keys and a faster beat
      - **To calm down:** slow tempos, soft timbres, gentle dynamics
      - **To feel understood:** songs whose lyrics match your story

      Start with the feeling you want, then pick the ingredients that create it.
    notes: Ask the audience what song changes their mood fastest.

  - layout: recap
    title: Recap
    body: |
      - Music changes mood through prediction and dopamine
      - Tempo, key, timbre, dynamics, lyrics and memory all matter
      - Fast and major usually energise; slow and minor usually soothe
      - Familiar music links feeling to memory
      - You can choose music to steer how you feel